1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,400
Here's a result that nearly fooled a student.

2
00:00:02,800 --> 00:00:05,360
She's testing unknown C for chloride.

3
00:00:05,760 --> 00:00:08,480
She acidifies a few drops with nitric acid,

4
00:00:08,640 --> 00:00:11,360
adds silver nitrate, and a faint white cloud

5
00:00:11,480 --> 00:00:12,000
appears.

6
00:00:12,480 --> 00:00:13,200
Chloride?

7
00:00:13,760 --> 00:00:15,680
But in the next well, she'd put plain

8
00:00:15,760 --> 00:00:18,320
water and the same reagents, and that well

9
00:00:18,400 --> 00:00:19,360
went cloudy too.

10
00:00:19,840 --> 00:00:21,920
If the water does it, the cloud can't

11
00:00:22,040 --> 00:00:23,840
prove anything about the unknown.

12
00:00:24,240 --> 00:00:26,320
She'd rinsed the plate with tap water, and

13
00:00:26,400 --> 00:00:28,560
tap water carries a little chloride.

14
00:00:29,040 --> 00:00:31,920
That one extra well saved her conclusion.

15
00:00:33,134 --> 00:00:36,546
The aim: identify the cation and the anion

16
00:00:36,654 --> 00:00:39,693
in each of four unknown solutions, where each

17
00:00:39,894 --> 00:00:42,373
holds one of each from the syllabus lists.

18
00:00:42,814 --> 00:00:44,973
Plan before you pick up a dropper: list

19
00:00:45,054 --> 00:00:47,613
the tests in order, from the two flowcharts,

20
00:00:47,694 --> 00:00:49,773
and what each result would mean.

21
00:00:49,934 --> 00:00:52,493
In every row of wells, only the solution

22
00:00:53,054 --> 00:00:53,453
changes.

23
00:00:53,614 --> 00:00:56,413
That's your independent variable, and what you see

24
00:00:56,494 --> 00:00:58,293
is the dependent variable.

25
00:00:58,494 --> 00:01:00,013
Everything else stays the same.

26
00:01:01,234 --> 00:01:02,553
Now the controls.

27
00:01:02,754 --> 00:01:06,113
A blank, or negative control, is distilled water

28
00:01:06,274 --> 00:01:07,313
with the reagent.

29
00:01:07,714 --> 00:01:09,433
It should show no change.

30
00:01:09,634 --> 00:01:12,193
If it does, the reagent or the equipment

31
00:01:12,274 --> 00:01:14,673
is adding something, like that tap water.

32
00:01:15,154 --> 00:01:18,273
A known, or positive control, is a solution

33
00:01:18,394 --> 00:01:20,593
you know holds the ion, with the same

34
00:01:20,674 --> 00:01:21,473
reagent.

35
00:01:21,714 --> 00:01:24,193
It shows the test works today, and what

36
00:01:24,274 --> 00:01:26,233
a positive looks like on your plate.

37
00:01:26,594 --> 00:01:30,433
So each reagent gets one row: blank, known,

38
00:01:30,514 --> 00:01:31,553
unknown.

39
00:01:31,634 --> 00:01:34,673
And rinse everything with distilled water, never tap

40
00:01:34,754 --> 00:01:35,153
water.

41
00:01:36,367 --> 00:01:37,406
Run it small.

42
00:01:37,887 --> 00:01:39,646
A well plate, like the one from the

43
00:01:39,727 --> 00:01:43,166
solubility practical in Module 5, takes three drops

44
00:01:43,207 --> 00:01:45,766
of solution and three of reagent.

45
00:01:46,047 --> 00:01:48,446
That means less silver and barium to handle,

46
00:01:48,527 --> 00:01:49,406
and less waste.

47
00:01:50,047 --> 00:01:52,206
Keep the dropper tip above the well, so

48
00:01:52,287 --> 00:01:54,606
it never carries one solution back into a

49
00:01:54,687 --> 00:01:55,326
bottle.

50
00:01:55,647 --> 00:01:57,566
Put a black card under the wells to

51
00:01:57,647 --> 00:01:59,966
see white solids, and a white tile to

52
00:02:00,047 --> 00:02:01,006
judge colours.

53
00:02:02,234 --> 00:02:05,993
Unknown C, cation first, fresh drops in each

54
00:02:06,074 --> 00:02:06,473
well.

55
00:02:07,034 --> 00:02:10,713
Dilute hydrochloric acid: no visible change, which rules

56
00:02:10,794 --> 00:02:13,073
out silver, and usually lead.

57
00:02:13,354 --> 00:02:17,553
Dilute sulfuric acid: no change, so not barium.

58
00:02:17,834 --> 00:02:21,273
Sodium hydroxide: a white precipitate that stays when

59
00:02:21,314 --> 00:02:22,313
you add more.

60
00:02:22,394 --> 00:02:24,953
That points to magnesium, or calcium from a

61
00:02:25,034 --> 00:02:26,473
concentrated sample.

62
00:02:26,594 --> 00:02:28,233
So, a flame test.

63
00:02:28,314 --> 00:02:30,153
Clean the loop in acid and heat it

64
00:02:30,274 --> 00:02:32,233
until the flame shows no colour.

65
00:02:32,314 --> 00:02:33,913
That's your blank flame.

66
00:02:34,074 --> 00:02:36,233
Known calcium on the same loop gives brick

67
00:02:36,314 --> 00:02:37,033
red.

68
00:02:37,154 --> 00:02:40,153
Clean again, then the sample: still no colour,

69
00:02:40,274 --> 00:02:42,313
so calcium isn't supported.

70
00:02:42,354 --> 00:02:45,193
It's magnesium, and the known magnesium well looks

71
00:02:45,274 --> 00:02:46,153
just the same.

72
00:02:47,367 --> 00:02:50,366
Then the anion, again with fresh drops.

73
00:02:50,647 --> 00:02:54,686
Dilute nitric acid: no fizz, so no carbonate.

74
00:02:54,887 --> 00:02:58,806
Nitric acid, then barium chloride: a white precipitate

75
00:02:58,887 --> 00:03:01,606
that stays in the acid, so sulfate.

76
00:03:01,687 --> 00:03:04,886
Nitric acid, then silver nitrate: with the plate

77
00:03:04,927 --> 00:03:07,846
rinsed in distilled water, the blank stays clear,

78
00:03:07,927 --> 00:03:09,846
and so does unknown C.

79
00:03:09,927 --> 00:03:11,526
No chloride after all.

80
00:03:11,647 --> 00:03:15,126
Each bottle holds one anion, so unknown C

81
00:03:15,207 --> 00:03:16,726
is magnesium sulfate.

82
00:03:17,934 --> 00:03:20,173
Record as you go, in a table you

83
00:03:20,227 --> 00:03:22,013
drew up before you started.

84
00:03:22,134 --> 00:03:25,613
One row per well: the solution, the reagent,

85
00:03:25,694 --> 00:03:28,013
what you saw, and only then, in its

86
00:03:28,094 --> 00:03:30,333
own column, what it means.

87
00:03:30,494 --> 00:03:32,573
Write each observation in full.

88
00:03:32,814 --> 00:03:36,733
No visible change, written in, never left blank.

89
00:03:36,814 --> 00:03:39,373
Note anything that changes with time, like a

90
00:03:39,534 --> 00:03:41,533
green solid that browns in air.

91
00:03:42,734 --> 00:03:45,533
When you report an identity, give the evidence

92
00:03:45,614 --> 00:03:46,653
and the chemistry.

93
00:03:47,054 --> 00:03:50,733
For magnesium: sodium hydroxide gave a white precipitate

94
00:03:50,814 --> 00:03:54,333
that stayed in excess, magnesium ions and hydroxide

95
00:03:54,414 --> 00:03:58,333
ions form solid magnesium hydroxide, the colourless flame

96
00:03:58,494 --> 00:04:01,693
doesn't support calcium, and the known matched.

97
00:04:02,174 --> 00:04:05,213
One more honest limit: no visible change means

98
00:04:05,294 --> 00:04:06,173
not detected.

99
00:04:06,574 --> 00:04:09,533
A very dilute ion can slip past, so

100
00:04:09,614 --> 00:04:11,853
say what your tests can and can't rule

101
00:04:11,934 --> 00:04:12,333
out.

102
00:04:13,534 --> 00:04:15,613
Now the three words from the practical skills

103
00:04:15,694 --> 00:04:16,413
video.

104
00:04:16,734 --> 00:04:17,373
Valid?

105
00:04:17,614 --> 00:04:20,013
The sequence should identify the ion it's meant

106
00:04:20,134 --> 00:04:20,333
to.

107
00:04:20,814 --> 00:04:24,733
Nitric acid before silver, fresh drops, distilled water

108
00:04:24,814 --> 00:04:27,213
and a clear blank all protect that.

109
00:04:27,454 --> 00:04:28,293
Reliable?

110
00:04:28,494 --> 00:04:31,293
Repeat each deciding test with fresh drops, and

111
00:04:31,374 --> 00:04:32,733
compare with another group.

112
00:04:33,134 --> 00:04:33,893
Accurate?

113
00:04:34,094 --> 00:04:37,053
Here, the result is an identity, so accurate

114
00:04:37,134 --> 00:04:38,493
means it's the true one.

115
00:04:38,974 --> 00:04:40,973
Matching the known side by side gets you

116
00:04:41,054 --> 00:04:43,213
close, and the teacher's key at the end

117
00:04:43,294 --> 00:04:44,013
checks it.

118
00:04:45,234 --> 00:04:46,593
Now the risks.

119
00:04:46,914 --> 00:04:50,513
Silver nitrate stains skin brown-black for days and

120
00:04:50,594 --> 00:04:53,913
can damage eyes, so wear gloves and glasses.

121
00:04:54,194 --> 00:04:56,433
Lead salts are toxic and may harm an

122
00:04:56,514 --> 00:04:59,793
unborn child, so lead results come as data,

123
00:04:59,834 --> 00:05:01,593
and you don't handle lead.

124
00:05:01,874 --> 00:05:04,593
Barium salts are toxic if swallowed, so your

125
00:05:04,754 --> 00:05:07,153
teacher prepares the dilute solution and you use

126
00:05:07,234 --> 00:05:08,513
it by the drop.

127
00:05:08,594 --> 00:05:11,793
Sodium hydroxide and dilute nitric acid can burn

128
00:05:11,874 --> 00:05:13,313
eyes and skin.

129
00:05:13,394 --> 00:05:16,113
Leave concentrated ammonia to the fume cupboard, and

130
00:05:16,194 --> 00:05:18,113
let the hot loop cool on a heatproof

131
00:05:18,274 --> 00:05:18,913
mat.

132
00:05:18,994 --> 00:05:21,953
Silver residues go into the silver waste, barium

133
00:05:22,034 --> 00:05:24,673
into heavy-metal waste, never the sink.

134
00:05:25,900 --> 00:05:30,500
One line for every reagent: blank, known, unknown.

135
00:05:30,780 --> 00:05:33,620
The blank shows the reagent adds nothing.

136
00:05:33,900 --> 00:05:36,140
The known shows what a positive looks like.

137
00:05:36,620 --> 00:05:39,260
Then the unknown is judged against both.

138
00:05:40,467 --> 00:05:41,106
Your turn.

139
00:05:41,667 --> 00:05:45,026
A student flame-tests four unknowns with one wire

140
00:05:45,107 --> 00:05:48,226
loop, rinsing it in water between samples.

141
00:05:48,307 --> 00:05:51,506
All four flames are blue-green, and she concludes

142
00:05:51,747 --> 00:05:53,746
all four contain copper.

143
00:05:54,027 --> 00:05:57,266
Evaluate her conclusion, and propose an improvement.

144
00:05:57,387 --> 00:05:58,626
Pause here to answer.

145
00:06:09,034 --> 00:06:09,833
Not valid.

146
00:06:10,314 --> 00:06:12,633
A water rinse may not clean copper off

147
00:06:12,674 --> 00:06:15,193
the loop, so copper from the first sample

148
00:06:15,274 --> 00:06:17,673
could colour every later flame.

149
00:06:17,874 --> 00:06:20,393
Before each sample, clean the loop in dilute

150
00:06:20,474 --> 00:06:23,273
hydrochloric acid and heat it until the flame

151
00:06:23,354 --> 00:06:24,793
shows no colour.

152
00:06:25,074 --> 00:06:27,993
Then confirm with a precipitate: pale blue with

153
00:06:28,074 --> 00:06:29,673
sodium hydroxide.

154
00:06:29,754 --> 00:06:32,553
Just repeating the flame tests is the trap:

155
00:06:32,634 --> 00:06:35,353
the same dirty loop gives the same result.

156
00:06:36,567 --> 00:06:37,206
Last one.

157
00:06:37,847 --> 00:06:40,246
Unknown E's results are on the card, with

158
00:06:40,287 --> 00:06:41,686
the blanks and knowns.

159
00:06:42,167 --> 00:06:46,326
Identify both ions, justify each identity, and write

160
00:06:46,487 --> 00:06:48,246
one net ionic equation.

161
00:06:48,727 --> 00:06:50,006
Pause here to answer.

162
00:07:00,434 --> 00:07:01,633
Calcium chloride.

163
00:07:01,714 --> 00:07:05,233
The white sulfate means barium or calcium, and

164
00:07:05,314 --> 00:07:08,673
the brick-red flame, matching the known, decides it:

165
00:07:08,834 --> 00:07:09,313
calcium.

166
00:07:09,794 --> 00:07:12,433
For the anion, a white silver precipitate in

167
00:07:12,514 --> 00:07:15,393
nitric acid that dissolves in dilute ammonia, with

168
00:07:15,447 --> 00:07:18,993
a clear blank, supports chloride: silver ions and

169
00:07:19,074 --> 00:07:22,073
chloride ions form solid silver chloride.

170
00:07:22,354 --> 00:07:25,473
Writing only "tested with silver nitrate" is the

171
00:07:25,554 --> 00:07:26,353
slip.

172
00:07:26,434 --> 00:07:27,633
Say what you saw.

173
00:07:28,834 --> 00:07:30,513
So, for any unknown:

174
00:07:31,154 --> 00:07:34,433
plan the tests from the flowcharts, run blank,

175
00:07:34,514 --> 00:07:37,153
known and unknown side by side at small

176
00:07:37,234 --> 00:07:40,113
scale, record what you see before what it

177
00:07:40,194 --> 00:07:43,873
means, and justify each identity with an observation

178
00:07:44,034 --> 00:07:44,993
and an equation.

179
00:07:46,194 --> 00:07:48,513
Next: you know which ion is there.

180
00:07:48,594 --> 00:07:49,793
But how much?

181
00:07:50,114 --> 00:07:51,473
Colour and light can tell you.

182
00:07:52,700 --> 00:07:54,140
Here are the key notes.

183
00:07:54,380 --> 00:07:56,300
Pause the video if you need more time

184
00:07:56,380 --> 00:07:57,180
to copy them.
